    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cda34b57-6898-59b8-a904-2778fa3418dc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{x}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x^{2}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
